                    briefing_text_r2:       `Strategy and the Phase-1 plan are due to the board in six weeks. Resist the urge to fix everything. Choose a <strong>coherent few</strong> priorities and ensure each is aligned to <em>how SMF actually competes</em> for that at-risk customer and what the customer requires. The trap is a long list of functional wish-items — stand up S&OP, redesign the network, restore service, release working capital — with no thread tying them to competitive position. A priority chosen without that alignment optimizes the wrong thing: chase plant-cost reduction and you'll cut cost while the service that wins the $275M account keeps slipping.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Sequence: phase the $40M for early, provable value`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Allocate the <strong>$40M</strong> across the initiative slate and sequence it under the board gate. A full single-ERP unification alone is ~$28M and 24+ months — it would consume the budget and deliver <em>zero service improvement</em> before the customer's second review. The discipline is sequencing for early value: which low-cost / high-impact moves — integrated S&OP, a network fix, working-capital release — <strong>fund and de-risk</strong> the bigger bets? And remember the enablers: buying automation and software while your planning team is still five people in spreadsheets is how you end up with an expensive system nobody can run. Name your deliberate Phase-1 first move.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend: the board brief & Phase-1 gate`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `You are in the room with the CEO and the Audit/Finance committee. Show that Phase 1 <strong>restores service</strong> to the at-risk customer <em>and</em> releases cash quickly enough to fund Phase 2 — the cash you're already holding in your own warehouses. The board approves <strong>gates, not hope</strong>: every phase needs a measurable success test — an OTIF target, a cash-released target, a date — and an honest answer to “what if Phase 1 underdelivers?”. A gate without a test isn't governance. Lock in your Phase-1 commitment and earn the buy-in.`,

