                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_strategy:      `<strong>That's the reframe.</strong> The board didn't hire you to clean up operations — it hired you because SMF has no integrated supply-chain strategy, just functional plans that don't add up. Naming the gap as a <em>strategy</em> gap, not an ops gap, is the first of Dittmann's five steps. Now point it at the threat that actually defines value.`,
                    coach_r1_operations:    `<strong>'Tighten the plants and the numbers recover' is the trap.</strong> The plants already optimize locally — that's exactly why the network underperforms. This is a strategy gap, not an operations clean-up. If you frame it as ops, you'll keep cutting cost while the integrated strategy never gets built.`,
                    coach_r1_it:            `<strong>Three ERPs is a symptom, not the strategy.</strong> Unify the technology first and you'll spend ~$28M and two years before anything improves — and your customer's review is in two quarters. The fragmentation is real, but it's not where you start.`,
                    coach_r1_people:        `<strong>Better planners help, but they're not the strategy.</strong> Five great people in spreadsheets with no S&OP cadence still can't run an integrated network. People are an enabler — name the strategy gap first.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You framed it as a strategy gap AND named service to the $275M customer as the value-defining threat. That's the integrated lens: the strategy exists to protect how SMF wins this account. Everything you sequence next should serve that.`,
                    coach_r1_cost:          `<strong>You named cost as the threat — but cost isn't what you're about to lose.</strong> The value-defining issue is <em>service</em> to a customer worth $275M, a strike from delisting. Optimize cost while service slips and you'll keep the business right up until you lose it.`,
                    coach_r1_lowposition:   `<strong>A diagnosis untied to how SMF competes drifts into a generic ops review.</strong> Tie it to the customer requirement — service — or your priorities next round will optimize the wrong thing.`,
                    coach_r2_service:       `<strong>Service-led is the aligned choice.</strong> Restoring OTIF to the $275M customer via integrated S&OP and a network fix is how SMF wins this account — and it's the priority every other one should reinforce. Now keep the list short and link each priority to competitive position.`,
                    coach_r2_cost:          `<strong>Plant-cost reduction while service is the crisis is Common Error number three.</strong> Your value-defining issue is service to a $275M customer, and you're optimizing cost while the thing that wins the account gets worse. How does cost-cutting connect to keeping that customer?`,
                    coach_r2_erp:           `<strong>ERP-first as the lead priority delivers nothing before the review.</strong> It's a 24-month, budget-consuming program that doesn't move OTIF a single point before your customer's second service review. Lead with what restores service, not with the foundation that pays off in year three.`,
                    coach_r2_everything:    `<strong>'The company needs all of it' is how you blow the budget and the focus.</strong> The board approves gates, not wish-lists. Pursue everything and nothing moves fast enough to matter before the review. Pick the coherent few that fund and de-risk the rest.`,
                    coach_r2_allcount:      `<strong>Six-plus priorities isn't a strategy — it's a backlog.</strong> Resist the urge to fund every function. A coherent few, aligned to how SMF competes, beats a long list nobody can sequence or govern.`,
                    coach_r2_noalign:       `<strong>Priorities the board has to infer the strategy from won't survive the gate.</strong> Write the one-line link from each priority to competitive position and the customer requirement — that thread is what makes it a strategy, not a list.`,
                    coach_r3_erpfirst:      `<strong>You've put the budget into a single-ERP unification as the first move.</strong> Admirable foundation — but it's ~$28M and 24 months, and your largest customer's second review is in two quarters. The ERP delivers exactly zero OTIF improvement before then; you'll likely be delisted before go-live. What could you do in 90 days, for a fraction of the cost, that actually moves service?`,
                    coach_r3_sopfirst:      `<strong>S&OP-first is the textbook sequence.</strong> Integrated S&OP plus a quick network fix is cheap, moves OTIF and releases cash in ~90 days — early, provable value that funds and de-risks the bigger bets. That's the deliberate first move the board can gate on.`,
                    coach_r3_autofirst:     `<strong>Automation-first locks up capital for a slow payback.</strong> It's capital-heavy and slow to land — it does little for OTIF before the review and frees little cash. Sequence the fast, cheap service moves first; automation can wait for a later gate.`,
                    coach_r3_noenablers:    `<strong>You bought assets and skipped the enablers.</strong> Automation and software won't run themselves while your planning team is five people in spreadsheets with no S&OP cadence. The New Supply Chain Agenda says talent, collaboration and change management <em>make</em> the technology work. Who runs this?`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>A defensible spread</strong> — but tilt it toward the moves that prove value soonest. The more weight on S&OP, the network fix and working-capital release, and the less locked into a 24-month ERP up front, the stronger your Phase-1 gate case.`,
                    coach_r4_quantified:    `<strong>That's a gate the board can govern.</strong> A quantified test — OTIF to 94%+ and $25M+ released within two quarters — tells them exactly how they'll know Phase 1 worked before they approve Phase 2. A gate with a number is governance; a gate without one is hope.`,
                    coach_r4_directional:   `<strong>'Meaningful improvement' isn't a gate test.</strong> The board will ask: how will we know Phase 1 worked? What's the OTIF target, the cash number, the date? Put a number and a date on it or the gate can't be governed.`,
                    coach_r4_notest:        `<strong>No hard test isn't governance — it's hope.</strong> Every phase says 'transform' and the board has no way to know whether to fund the next one. Commit an OTIF target, a cash-released target and a date, or they can't approve the gate.`,
                    coach_r4_selffund:      `<strong>That's the conversation the CFO wanted.</strong> Phase 1's released working capital funds Phase 2, so you're asking the board for less new cash — you're spending money you were already holding in your own warehouses. A quantified gate plus a self-funding bridge is the credible board case.`,
                    coach_r4_askfund:       `<strong>You quantified the gate but then asked for the full new tranche anyway.</strong> Why ask the board for cash you're already releasing from inventory? Show the self-funding bridge — Phase 1's freed working capital paying for Phase 2 — and the case gets far stronger.`,
                    coach_r4_overconfident: `<strong>'It won't underdeliver' is not an answer the board accepts.</strong> They're governing risk, not buying optimism. Bring a pre-agreed contingency — a de-scoped fallback and a hold on Phase 2 spend if the gate test misses — and you'll keep their confidence even if Phase 1 stumbles.`,

