                    insight_integrated:     `Integrated & self-funding: you treated diagnosis, priorities, sequencing and governance as one strategy — S&OP-first restored service to the $275M customer, working-capital release self-funded the next phase, and a quantified gate earned board buy-in. That's Dittmann's front end executed: an integrated strategy, not a list of functional wish-items.`,
                    insight_erp:            `ERP-first big bang: you put the budget and 24 months into a single ERP that delivered zero OTIF improvement before the customer's second review. The foundation may have been right in the abstract, but the sequencing wasn't — by go-live you'd likely have been delisted. Sequencing for early value, not scope, was the survival skill.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully reconcile the five steps. The diagnosis, priorities, sequencing and gate still tell slightly different stories — enough that the board can't see the integrated strategy and the customer's clock keeps running.`,
                    insight_otif_low:       `OTIF stayed below the 94% recovery line: the moves you sequenced didn't restore service to the at-risk customer fast enough. On a $275M account a strike from delisting, service is the value-defining metric — and it had to move before the second review.`,
                    insight_otif_recovered: `OTIF recovered toward the required 96%: integrated S&OP and the network fix restored service to the at-risk customer — the value-defining outcome that keeps the $275M account.`,
                    insight_cash_trapped:   `You left cash trapped: little of the ~$60M+ excess inventory and the 94-day cash-to-cash cycle was released. That's the self-funding lever you didn't pull — the cash you were already holding could have funded Phase 2 instead of asking the board for more.`,
                    insight_cash_freed:     `You released real working capital: freeing inventory off the 94-day cash-to-cash cycle self-funded the next phase and changed the budget conversation with the CFO — exactly the lever the New Supply Chain Agenda prizes.`,
                    insight_nogate:         `You presented a roadmap with no measurable gate test. Without an OTIF target, a cash number and a date, the board can't govern the transformation — a gate without a success test isn't governance, it's hope.`,
                    insight_misaligned:     `Your priorities optimized cost while service to the $275M customer was the value-defining issue. A supply chain has no 'best' design in the abstract — it must serve how the company wins, and yours chased the wrong metric.`,

                    hint_erp:               `You've sequenced an ERP-first big bang. It consumes the budget and delivers no service before the customer's second review — rebalance toward S&OP, a network fix and working-capital release that move OTIF in 90 days.`,
                    hint_misaligned:        `Your strategy is optimizing cost while service is the crisis. Re-anchor every priority to how SMF wins the $275M account — service-led, not cost-led.`,
                    hint_cash:              `Little cash released so far. Pull the self-funding lever — inventory optimization on the 94-day cash-to-cash cycle frees the ~$60M+ you're already holding and funds Phase 2.`,
                    hint_exposure:          `Delisting exposure is dangerously high — the service clock to the $275M customer is running out. Sequence the moves that lift OTIF fastest and lead the board with a service-restoring Phase 1.`,
                    hint_otif:              `OTIF is still below the recovery line with little time left. Fund and sequence integrated S&OP and the network fix — they move service fastest for the least money.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — OTIF recovering, board confidence high and exposure low. Lock in a quantified, self-funded Phase-1 gate to hold it.`,

